    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_22_resistance_formula",
      "card_slug": "card_22_resistance_formula",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "resistance_formula",
      "level": 3,
      "progress": {
        "current": 22,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Formula hai: R = ρL/A. Jahan R = resistance (Ω), ρ = resistivity of material (Ω·m), L = length (m), A = area of cross-section (m²). Resistivity ek material property hai - copper ka kam (good conductor), rubber ka bahut zyada (insulator)!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Toh copper wire electricity ke liye best hai?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Haan! Copper aur aluminium conductors mein use hote hain kyunki inki resistivity bahut kam hai. ⭐ BPSC TRE Favorite - Silver sabse best conductor hai (lowest resistivity) lekin expensive. Copper practical choice hai! Insulators: rubber, plastic, glass - high resistivity!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Sir Ohm ka law kya hai?"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_23_ohms_law_intro",
        "button_text": "Ohm's Law ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_23_ohms_law_intro",
      "card_slug": "card_23_ohms_law_intro",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "ohms_law",
      "level": 3,
      "progress": {
        "current": 23,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Ye electricity ka GOLDEN LAW hai! OHM'S LAW: At constant temperature, current through a conductor is directly proportional to potential difference across it. Formula: V = IR ya I = V/R ya R = V/I. Sabse important relationship!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Matlab agar voltage double karein toh current bhi double ho jayega?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Bilkul sahi! (Provided resistance constant rahe aur temperature bhi). ⭐ NEET/SSC High-Weightage Formula! Graph banao V vs I - straight line milegi (slope = R). Ye linear relationship sabse zyada questions mein aata hai!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Example se samjhaiye Sir!"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_24_ohms_law_example",
        "button_text": "Example Dekho ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_24_ohms_law_example",
      "card_slug": "card_24_ohms_law_example",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "ohms_law_example",
      "level": 3,
      "progress": {
        "current": 24,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Dekho: Agar ek bulb ka resistance 100 Ω hai aur tum usko 220V supply se connect karo, toh current kitna flow hoga? Simple: I = V/R = 220/100 = 2.2 Ampere! Agar resistance 200 Ω hota toh current = 220/200 = 1.1 A - half!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Matlab zyada resistance = kam current!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Perfect understanding! Isliye ghar mein high-power devices (heater, AC) kam resistance ke hote hain - zyada current kheenche. Low-power devices (LED bulb) zyada resistance. ⭐ Railway/SSC Application Question Favorite!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Ye toh practical hai!"
        }
      ],
